CHMOD(2)                       P32W Unix                        CHMOD(2)

NAME
       chmod - change the mode of a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/stat.h>

       int chmod(const char *path, int mode);

DESCRIPTION
       chmod sets the permission bits of the file path to mode
       (masked with 07777); the file type does not change. Only the
       file's owner or the super-user may do it. The bits:

       04000  set user ID on execution
       02000  set group ID on execution
       01000  sticky: in a directory, only the owner of a file
              (or the super-user) may remove or rename it
       0400   read by owner       0040   read by group
       0200   write by owner      0020   write by group
       0100   execute (search)    0010   execute by group
              by owner
       0004   read by others      0002   write by others
       0001   execute by others

       The file's change time is set to now.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EPERM   The caller is neither the owner nor the
               super-user.
       ENOENT  path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path.
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       chown(2), stat(2), umask(2), chmod(1)

BUGS
       Any owner may set the sticky and set-group-ID bits, whatever
       the file's group (V7 kept the sticky bit for the
       super-user). There is no fchmod.
